WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.845
A two-four tree stays balanced

2
00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:06.270
without rotations.

3
00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.630
But a node with one, two, or three keys

4
00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:10.830
and up to four children

5
00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.350
is awkward to store.

6
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:14.590
It is a list of variable length

7
00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:15.940
in every node.

8
00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:17.953
What if we could draw the same tree

9
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.240
with ordinary binary nodes

10
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.660
each with one key and two children?

11
00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.170
We can, with a colour.

12
00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.910
This is the red-black tree.

13
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.815
We'll see how a two-four tree

14
00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:31.670
becomes a binary tree

15
00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:34.130
where the three red-black rules come from

16
00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:35.970
how insertion works in this picture

17
00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:38.180
and how tall the tree can get.

18
00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.030
A two-node has one key.

19
00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:43.480
It is simply one black node.

20
00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.100
A three-node has two keys.

21
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.140
Write it as a black node with one red child

22
00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.230
on the left or on the right.

23
00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.910
A four-node has three keys.

24
00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.850
Write it as a black node

25
00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.820
with a red child on each side.

26
00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.080
So a red node means

27
00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.460
I am a key that shares my parent's node.

28
00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:06.610
Everything black is the top of a node.

29
00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:08.880
Now a whole tree.

30
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.580
Take this two-four tree

31
00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:13.540
twenty alone on top, ten on the left

32
00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.350
and thirty and forty together on the right.

33
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.750
Switch to the binary picture.

34
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.530
Twenty, black.

35
00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.730
Ten, black.

36
00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:26.340
Thirty, black, with forty as its red child.

37
00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.205
The red-black rules can now be read

38
00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:30.950
straight off the picture.

39
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:32.257
The root is black

40
00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:33.778
because the root of the tree

41
00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:35.880
is the top of a node.

42
00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:39.171
A red node never has a red child.

43
00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.513
A red node is a key inside

44
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:42.680
its parent's node

45
00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.350
and a node has at most three keys.

46
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.882
Every path from the root down to

47
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:49.738
a missing child passes through the

48
00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.468
same number of black nodes.

49
00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:53.519
Each black node is one level

50
00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:54.640
of the two-four tree

51
00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:56.707
and all the leaves of a two-four

52
00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:58.700
tree are at the same depth.

53
00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.370
To insert

54
00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:03.210
do an ordinary binary search tree insertion

55
00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:05.210
and paint the new node red.

56
00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:07.200
It joins its parent's node.

57
00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:10.820
If the parent is black, the node had room

58
00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:12.510
and we are done.

59
00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:14.850
If the parent is red

60
00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.990
we have two reds in a row

61
00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:18.710
and we look at the uncle

62
00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:20.060
the parent's sibling.

63
00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:22.336
A red uncle means the two-four

64
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.900
node now has four keys.

65
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.300
Recolour the parent and the uncle black

66
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:28.940
and the grandparent red.

67
00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:31.380
That is exactly a split

68
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:34.330
the middle key moves up into the next node.

69
00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.213
A black uncle means the node is a three-node

70
00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:38.969
written the wrong way round.

71
00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.008
One or two rotations rewrite the

72
00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:42.990
same node in the right shape.

73
00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:45.720
The two-four tree does not change at all.

74
00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:50.390
Insert ten, twenty, thirty.

75
00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:53.830
Twenty is black, ten and thirty are red.

76
00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:56.450
Thirty makes a red-red line

77
00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:59.070
and one rotation rewrites it.

78
00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.590
In the two-four tree

79
00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:03.180
that was one four-node all along.

80
00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.080
Insert forty.

81
00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.440
The uncle is red.

82
00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.380
Recolour, and the four-node splits.

83
00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:14.240
Insert fifty, and a rotation.

84
00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.050
Insert twenty-five, and another recolouring.

85
00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:23.370
Six keys, two recolourings, two rotations

86
00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.880
and a tree of four levels.

87
00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.198
Deletion is the two-four

88
00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:29.830
deletion in disguise.

89
00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:31.770
Removing a red leaf is free

90
00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.820
it was only one key inside a bigger node.

91
00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:36.776
Removing a black node can

92
00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.640
leave a node empty, an underflow.

93
00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:41.471
Then there are the same two repairs.

94
00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.881
A transfer from a sibling is a

95
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:45.780
recolouring and a rotation.

96
00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:48.120
A fuse is a recolouring

97
00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:51.250
and it may push the shortage one level up.

98
00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:53.400
Pause and predict.

99
00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.229
In the first video of this chapter

100
00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.291
we inserted the numbers one to a thousand

101
00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:59.740
in order into an AVL tree

102
00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.910
and got ten levels.

103
00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:05.390
How many levels will a red-black tree have?

104
00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:06.850
Ten, again?

105
00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:07.830
Seventeen?

106
00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.500
Or a thousand?

107
00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:11.140
Seventeen.

108
00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:13.060
Taller than the AVL tree

109
00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:14.651
but still logarithmic.

110
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.730
A red-black tree promises at most two times

111
00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:20.520
log base two of n plus one levels

112
00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:23.210
and the AVL tree promises less.

113
00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:25.980
Where does that bound come from?

114
00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.411
Count the black nodes on any path.

115
00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.044
That is the number of levels

116
00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.265
of the two-four tree

117
00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:34.858
and a two-four tree with n keys has at most

118
00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:37.910
log base two of n plus one levels.

119
00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.010
Between two black nodes there

120
00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:42.250
can be at most one red node

121
00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:44.713
because two reds in a row are not allowed.

122
00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:47.399
So a path is at most twice as

123
00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:48.700
long as its black count.

124
00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:51.133
The height is at most two times

125
00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:53.980
log base two of n plus one.

126
00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:57.280
For a hundred thousand keys, in order

127
00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.860
the AVL tree has seventeen levels

128
00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:02.300
and the red-black tree thirty-one

129
00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.290
below the limit of thirty-three.

130
00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.550
What does the red-black tree get in return?

131
00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:08.650
Cheaper updates.

132
00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:11.370
An insertion needs at most two rotations

133
00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:13.520
and a deletion at most three.

134
00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.091
To sum up.

135
00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:17.465
A red-black tree is a two-four tree

136
00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:19.379
written with binary nodes.

137
00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:21.044
A black node and its red

138
00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:23.260
children form one node.

139
00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:27.460
Red uncle: a split, done with a recolouring.

140
00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:30.160
Black uncle: rewrite the node

141
00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:31.690
with rotations.

142
00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:35.950
The height is at most twice log n.

143
00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:38.610
And Java's tree map is a red-black tree.

144
00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:40.040
Let's see the code.

145
00:05:40.667 --> 00:05:43.337
Here is the red-black tree in Java

146
00:05:43.337 --> 00:05:45.417
with insertion, deletion

147
00:05:45.417 --> 00:05:48.787
and a conversion back to a two-four tree.

148
00:05:49.367 --> 00:05:53.157
A node has a key, a colour, two children

149
00:05:53.157 --> 00:05:54.357
and a parent link.

150
00:05:54.357 --> 00:05:57.337
The colour is a boolean: RED is true

151
00:05:57.337 --> 00:05:58.687
and BLACK is false.

152
00:05:59.037 --> 00:06:01.927
The color-of helper treats a missing child

153
00:06:01.927 --> 00:06:03.527
null, as black.

154
00:06:03.527 --> 00:06:04.947
A record, View

155
00:06:04.947 --> 00:06:07.117
will hold the two-four reading.

156
00:06:07.697 --> 00:06:10.027
Insert walks down with compare to

157
00:06:10.027 --> 00:06:13.377
attaches a red node, and calls fix insert.

158
00:06:13.727 --> 00:06:15.377
While the parent is red

159
00:06:15.377 --> 00:06:16.557
we look at the uncle.

160
00:06:16.557 --> 00:06:18.757
A red uncle: recolour

161
00:06:18.757 --> 00:06:20.537
and continue from the grandparent.

162
00:06:20.537 --> 00:06:23.977
A black uncle: rotate, once or twice

163
00:06:23.977 --> 00:06:26.247
swap two colours, and stop.

164
00:06:26.597 --> 00:06:29.277
The root is painted black at the end.

165
00:06:29.857 --> 00:06:32.358
Remove splices out the node

166
00:06:32.447 --> 00:06:34.533
using the successor if there

167
00:06:34.607 --> 00:06:35.547
are two children.

168
00:06:35.547 --> 00:06:37.667
If a black node left

169
00:06:37.667 --> 00:06:40.417
fix remove restores the black count.

170
00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:43.514
It is the textbook case analysis

171
00:06:43.597 --> 00:06:45.427
a black sibling with a red

172
00:06:45.497 --> 00:06:48.457
child is a transfer, with rotations.

173
00:06:48.457 --> 00:06:52.349
A black sibling with no red child is a fuse

174
00:06:52.437 --> 00:06:53.664
with a recolouring that

175
00:06:53.717 --> 00:06:55.327
pushes the problem up.

176
00:06:55.907 --> 00:06:58.683
to two four gathers a black node and

177
00:06:58.757 --> 00:07:00.577
its red children into a View

178
00:07:00.577 --> 00:07:02.987
a node with one to three keys.

179
00:07:03.337 --> 00:07:06.900
The tests convert random red-black trees

180
00:07:06.987 --> 00:07:08.677
and check that each is a

181
00:07:08.747 --> 00:07:10.387
valid two-four tree

182
00:07:10.387 --> 00:07:12.937
with the same keys in order.

183
00:07:13.517 --> 00:07:16.407
Same demo, same output as Python

184
00:07:16.407 --> 00:07:19.207
thirty and fifty are rotations

185
00:07:19.207 --> 00:07:22.157
forty and twenty-five are recolourings.

186
00:07:22.507 --> 00:07:25.777
A thousand twenty-three keys, in order

187
00:07:25.777 --> 00:07:27.147
eighteen levels.

188
00:07:27.497 --> 00:07:28.907
And the JDK?

189
00:07:28.907 --> 00:07:32.307
tree map and tree set are red-black trees.

190
00:07:32.307 --> 00:07:33.977
The documentation says so.

191
00:07:34.557 --> 00:07:37.387
Now remove ten, and then twenty

192
00:07:37.387 --> 00:07:38.727
from the six-key tree.

193
00:07:38.727 --> 00:07:40.427
In the two-four view

194
00:07:40.427 --> 00:07:42.087
removing ten empties a leaf

195
00:07:42.087 --> 00:07:44.347
and its sibling has two keys.

196
00:07:44.347 --> 00:07:47.387
That is a transfer: twenty comes down

197
00:07:47.387 --> 00:07:49.277
and twenty-five goes up.

198
00:07:49.627 --> 00:07:52.757
Removing twenty empties a leaf again

199
00:07:52.757 --> 00:07:56.297
but now the sibling has one key only.

200
00:07:56.297 --> 00:07:57.797
That is a fuse

201
00:07:57.797 --> 00:08:00.107
twenty-five comes down to join thirty.

202
00:08:00.457 --> 00:08:02.054
The red-black tree did it with

203
00:08:02.107 --> 00:08:03.947
rotations and recolourings.

204
00:08:03.947 --> 00:08:06.377
The two-four picture tells you why.

205
00:08:06.957 --> 00:08:08.847
Two tests matter most.

206
00:08:08.847 --> 00:08:11.865
One runs random inserts and removes

207
00:08:11.947 --> 00:08:13.310
three hundred operations

208
00:08:13.367 --> 00:08:15.087
on each of forty trees

209
00:08:15.087 --> 00:08:18.357
and calls check invariants after every step.

210
00:08:18.707 --> 00:08:20.953
The other converts random red-black

211
00:08:21.017 --> 00:08:22.897
trees into two-four views

212
00:08:22.897 --> 00:08:26.017
and checks every result: node sizes

213
00:08:26.017 --> 00:08:28.137
child counts, leaf depth

214
00:08:28.137 --> 00:08:29.477
and the keys in order.

215
00:08:29.477 --> 00:08:32.347
That is the correspondence, tested.

216
00:08:32.927 --> 00:08:34.417
Common mistakes.

217
00:08:34.417 --> 00:08:36.655
Forgetting to paint the root black.

218
00:08:36.717 --> 00:08:38.947
Calling a method on null instead

219
00:08:39.017 --> 00:08:40.687
of using color of.

220
00:08:41.037 --> 00:08:42.669
Forgetting to fix the parent

221
00:08:42.727 --> 00:08:44.227
link in a rotation.

222
00:08:44.227 --> 00:08:47.127
And mixing up identity and equality

223
00:08:47.127 --> 00:08:49.947
compare nodes with double equals

224
00:08:49.947 --> 00:08:51.247
the same object

225
00:08:51.247 --> 00:08:53.677
but compare keys with compare to.

226
00:08:54.027 --> 00:08:57.032
Our check invariants throws an assertion error

227
00:08:57.097 --> 00:08:59.137
when any of the three rules breaks

228
00:08:59.137 --> 00:09:02.347
and the tests call it after every operation.

229
00:09:02.927 --> 00:09:04.597
In the standard library

230
00:09:04.597 --> 00:09:06.437
tree map is a red-black tree.

231
00:09:06.437 --> 00:09:10.037
It gives you get, put, and remove in log n

232
00:09:10.037 --> 00:09:12.597
plus floor entry, ceiling entry

233
00:09:12.597 --> 00:09:13.447
and sub map.

234
00:09:13.797 --> 00:09:16.387
The next video is about those operations

235
00:09:16.387 --> 00:09:18.477
and about what Python does instead.

